<spell-notebook "icon-picker usage">
	<header level="1">
		Icon Picker
		<header>An icon picker lets people choose an icon by searching for it and clicking its glyph.</>
	</>
	<icon-picker-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#its-icons">Its icons</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it, where its icons come from, keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`value` is an icon's NAME, as `<ui-icon name>` takes it (`star`, `address book`);  `""` is none.

			- The attribute is the starting (and reset) value;  the `value` property is the live one.
			- Picking sends the cancelable `ui-changing` (`{ value }`) first:  a handler that sets `el.value` again wins;  then
			  `ui-changed` once it's in.
			- In a `<ui-form value>` it binds like any control;  a property editor draws one for every
			  `icon` attribute (a button's, a rating's).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-icon-picker").addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => {
			  document.querySelector("ui-button").icon = event.detail.value
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Its icons">
		<markdown>
			It offers every icon its page knows:  its nearest `<ui-root icons>`'s packs, else the page's
			(Font Awesome's free icons by default), one cell per glyph.

			- With no search, they're in groups:  Font Awesome's own categories, A-Z
			  (Accessibility, Alert, Alphabet, Animals ...), each under its heading.
			  - A glyph in several categories is in each, as on Font Awesome's site.
			  - Glyphs in none come last, under Other:  most brands, and every glyph of a pack of your own.
			  - The heading of the group at the top stays there as you scroll.
			- A search is one list:  names first (exact, then starting with what you typed, then holding it),
			  then Font Awesome's search terms.
			- The categories and the search terms are one file, loaded as a picker first opens:
			  until it's in, the grid is one list, A-Z.
			- With `<ui-root icons="fomantic">`, Fomantic's names go to the glyphs they draw there.
			- The grid draws only the rows in view:  each glyph is a file of its own, fetched as it scrolls in.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- On the field:  Enter, Space, Down or Up opens it;  a letter opens it searching for that letter.
			- In the panel, the focus stays in the search box:
			  - the arrows move through the grid, Enter picks;  Down and Up step over a group's heading
			  - Escape closes it, back on the field;  Tab closes it and moves on
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The field is a button that opens a listbox;  named by the picker's `<label for>` or
			  `aria-label`, then what it shows ("Icon, star").
			- The search box is a combobox over the grid, a listbox of options, each named for its icon,
			  the chosen one `aria-selected`.
			- The group headings are hidden from screen readers:  a listbox holds only options.
			- Each glyph's name is its tooltip too.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
